{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01Pew97uhyFUJJMiN1tCzneU", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://www.timeanddate.com/astronomy/equilux.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.timeanddate.com/news/astronomy/equilux-2022\"}, {\"url\": \"https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/equal-day-and-night-on-the-equinox-march/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.caymancompass.com/2021/09/28/equilux-the-true-equal-day-and-night/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://dictionary.obspm.fr/terms/equilux/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.kbtx.com/2024/09/25/equinox-versus-equilux/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.caymancompass.com/?p=546345\"}, {\"url\": \"https://ozgurnevres.com/day-night-equal-equinox-equilux/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://converttime.com/en/blog/equinox-explained\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T12:34:31.874Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "equilux neologism day and night exactly equal date", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_019PPE3WgPUz8rYifS67D5Gj", "content": [{"title": "www.timeanddate.com", "url": "https://www.timeanddate.com/astronomy/equilux.html"}, {"title": "equilux 2022", "url": "https://www.timeanddate.com/news/astronomy/equilux-2022"}, {"title": "Astronomy Essentials", "url": "https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/equal-day-and-night-on-the-equinox-march/"}, {"title": "Equilux: The true equal day and night", "url": "https://www.caymancompass.com/2021/09/28/equilux-the-true-equal-day-and-night/"}, {"title": "An Etymological Dictionary of Astronomy and Astrophysics", "url": "https://dictionary.obspm.fr/terms/equilux/"}, {"title": "Day and night: EQUILUX vs EQUINOX", "url": "https://www.kbtx.com/2024/09/25/equinox-versus-equilux/"}, {"title": "Equilux: The true equal day and night", "url": "https://www.caymancompass.com/?p=546345"}, {"title": "Day and Night Aren’t Equal on the Equinox: It’s Equilux", "url": "https://ozgurnevres.com/day-night-equal-equinox-equilux/"}, {"title": "Back to Blog", "url": "https://converttime.com/en/blog/equinox-explained"}]}, "The search results say \"equilux\" names the day when day and night are equal, and that the date depends on latitude, so there is no single calendar date.\n\n- **Meaning and origin:** One source calls it a new word for you: equilux. The word is used to describe the day on which day and night are equal. Another notes that the word is drawn from Latin for equal (equi) and light (lux).\n- **Not the equinox:** Equinoxes are often assumed to be the balance point, but the equilux is a different day. One astrophysicist's dictionary entry says the day and night are not equal at the equinox, and the gap comes from the Sun being a disk rather than a point (sunrise corresponds to the moment the top of the disk (and not its center) emerges out of the horizon).\n- **Timing:** Each year has two equiluxes. The equilux happens a few to several days after the autumn equinox, and a few to several days before the spring equinox.\n- **Latitude dependence:** At and near the equator, there is no equilux whatsoever, because the daylight period is over 12 hours long every day of the year. Timeanddate's table gives examples such as September equilux dates of Sep 25 at 60° N and Sep 26 at 40° N. A 2024 KBTX article gives a concrete pair: in Casper, Wyoming the equilux occurs a day earlier on Wednesday, September 25 than in Bryan-College Station, which falls on September 26.\n\nFor a specific city, the exact date should be checked against a sunrise/sunset calculator for that location, since these sources cover different years and use slightly different sunrise/sunset definitions."], "durationSeconds": 8.526500958, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01Pew97uhyFUJJMiN1tCzneU", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgDVnjQZtigqVWngrvXME", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**答案：Equilux**\n\n这个词由拉丁语 equi（相等）和 lux（光）组成，指白天和黑夜一样长的那一天。它和春分、秋分（equinox）不是同一天。分点那天白天其实稍长一些，因为日出、日落是按太阳圆盘的上边缘越过地平线来算的。Equilux 一年有两次：春季的在春分前几天，秋季的在秋分后几天。具体日期随纬度变化，赤道附近全年白天都超过 12 小时，所以没有 equilux。\n\n本次只搜了 1 次。\n\nSources:\n- [timeanddate.com – Equilux](https://www.timeanddate.com/astronomy/equilux.html)\n- [An Etymological Dictionary of Astronomy and Astrophysics – equilux](https://dictionary.obspm.fr/terms/equilux/)\n- [Cayman Compass – Equilux: The true equal day and night](https://www.caymancompass.com/2021/09/28/equilux-the-true-equal-day-and-night/)"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1166, "cache_read_input_tokens": 4298, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1166}, "output_tokens": 358, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T12:34:35.861Z", "request_id": "req_011CgDVnj7DYrL31jrJBs4gd"}
